Step 1: What is Package Management?

A package manager installs, updates, and removes software.

Examples:

Java
Git
Jenkins
Maven
Docker
Nginx

Think of it like an app store for Linux.

Step 2: Understand dnf, yum, and rpm
Command	Purpose	Common Use
dnf	Modern package manager	Amazon Linux 2023, RHEL 8+, Fedora
yum	Older package manager	Amazon Linux 2, CentOS 7, RHEL 7
rpm	Install/query individual RPM packages	Manual package management
Step 3: Check Your Operating System
cat /etc/os-release

Example output:

NAME="Amazon Linux"
VERSION="2023"

If you're using Amazon Linux 2023, use dnf.

If you're using Amazon Linux 2, use yum.

Step 4: Install Software Using dnf

Search for a package:

dnf search git

Install Git:

sudo dnf install git -y

Check the version:

git --version
Step 5: Update Packages

Update package information:

sudo dnf check-update

Upgrade installed packages:

sudo dnf upgrade -y
Step 6: Remove a Package
sudo dnf remove git -y

Reinstall if needed:

sudo dnf install git -y
Step 7: Using yum (Amazon Linux 2)

If your server is Amazon Linux 2:

sudo yum install git -y

Other useful commands:

sudo yum update -y
sudo yum remove git -y
yum search java
Step 8: Working with RPM Packages

Check installed packages:

rpm -qa

Search:

rpm -qa | grep java

Show package details:

rpm -qi git

List files installed by a package:

rpm -ql git

Install a local RPM:

sudo rpm -ivh package.rpm

Note: dnf/yum are usually preferred because they automatically install dependencies, while rpm alone does not.

Step 9: Network Connectivity Commands
Check connectivity
ping google.com

Stop with:

Ctrl + C
Download a file

Using wget:

wget https://example.com/file.txt

Using curl:

curl https://example.com

Download and save a file:

curl -O https://example.com/file.txt
Step 10: Check IP Address
ip addr

Show only IPv4 addresses:

ip -4 addr
Step 11: Check Routing Table
ip route

This shows the default gateway and network routes.

Step 12: Check Open Ports

Show listening TCP and UDP ports:

ss -tuln

Find a specific port:

ss -tuln | grep 8080

For Jenkins (default port 8080), this tells you whether Jenkins is listening.

Step 13: Test Connectivity to a Port

If nc (netcat) is installed:

nc -zv localhost 8080

Or use curl for web services:

curl http://localhost:8080
Real-Time Production Scenario 1
Jenkins installation fails

Error: Package not found.

Troubleshooting
Check internet connectivity:
ping google.com
Refresh package metadata:
sudo dnf check-update
Verify repositories are enabled.
Real-Time Production Scenario 2
Jenkins UI is not accessible

Check if Jenkins is running:

systemctl status jenkins

Check if port 8080 is listening:

ss -tuln | grep 8080

If the service is running but the port isn't accessible:

Check the firewall.
Check the cloud security group (AWS/Azure/GCP).
Confirm Jenkins is configured to listen on the expected port.
Real-Time Production Scenario 3
Cannot clone a GitHub repository

Check internet access:

ping github.com

Test HTTPS:

curl -I https://github.com

If HTTPS works but Git fails, investigate:

Git credentials or SSH keys.
Proxy settings (if applicable).
Repository permissions.
